¶
'''belection''' (be-lek′shọn), ''n.'' Same as ''bolection.''

'''belecture''' (bẹ̄-lek′t̮ṳ̄r), ''v. t.'' [< ''be-''<sup>1</sup> + ''lecture.''] To vex with lectures: admonish persistently.
{{smaller block|She now had somebody, or rather something, to lecture and ''belecture'' as before. ''Savage,'' Reuben Medlicott, I. xvi.}}

'''belee'''<sup>1</sup> (bē-lē′), ''v. t.'' [< ''be-''<sup>1</sup> + ''lee''<sup>1</sup>.] To place on the lee, or in a position unfavorable to the wind. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>I . . . must be ''belee’d'' and calm’d
By debitor and creditor. ''Shak.,'' Othello, i. 1.</poem>}}

'''belee'''<sup>2</sup>†, ''v. t.'' An apocopated form of ''beleeve,'' now written ''believe.''
{{smaller block|<poem>''Fool.'' ''Belee'' me, sir.
''Chi.'' I would I could, sir! ''Fletcher,'' Mad Lover, v. 4.</poem>}}

