an appellative of Satan (2 Cor. vi. 15). But the Heb. ''b’līya‘al'' is a common noun, meaning worthlessness or wickedness; < ''b’lī'', without, + ''ya‘al'', use, profit.] The spirit of evil personified; the devil; Satan; in Milton, one of the fallen angels, distinct from Satan.

{{smaller block|What concord hath Christ with ''Belial?'' {{right|2 Cor. vi. 15.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>''Belial'' came last, than whom a spirit more lewd
Fell not from heaven.</poem>{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 490.}}}}

'''belibel''' (bē-lī′bel), ''v. t.'' [< ''be-''<sup>1</sup> + ''libel.''] To libel or traduce.

'''Belideus''' (bē-lid′ē-us), ''n.'' [NL.] A genus of small flying phalangers, of the family ''Phalangistidæ;'' the sugar-squirrels. {{smaller|These little marsupials resemble flying-squirrels in superficial appearance, having a large parachute, large naked ears, long bushy tail, and very soft fur. There are several species, such as ''B. sciureus'', ''B. ariel'', and ''B. flaviventer'', inhabiting Australia, New Guinea, and some of the neighboring islands.}}

'''belie'''<sup>1</sup>† (bē-lī′), ''v. t.''; pret. ''belay'', pp. ''belain'', ppr. ''belying.'' [< ME. ''belyen'', ''beliggen'', < AS. ''belicgan'', ''bilicgan'' (= OHG. ''biligan'', MHG. ''biligen'', G. ''beliegen''), < ''be-'', about, by, + ''licgan'', lie: see ''be-''<sup>1</sup> and ''lie''<sup>1</sup>, and cf. ''belay.''] To lie around; encompass; especially, to lie around, as an army; beleaguer.

'''belie'''<sup>2</sup> (bē-lī′), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''belied'', ppr. ''belying.'' [< ME. ''belyen'', ''beleogen'', < AS. ''beleógan'' (= OFries. ''biliaga'' = OHG. ''biliugan'', MHG. ''beliegen'', G. ''belügen''), < ''be-'', about, by, + ''leógan'', lie: see ''be-''<sup>1</sup> and ''lie''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' To tell lies concerning; calumniate by false reports.
